    "question": {
      ":question/id": "8cf3251c-433f-594d-83da-1302e2675cf6",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} + 9 y^{2} \\cos^{2}{(6 x )}=(- 3 y \\cos{(6 x )} + 6)^{2}\\,.\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} + 9 y^{2} \\cos^{2}{(6 x )}&=(- 3 y \\cos{(6 x )} + 6)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} + 9 y^{2} \\cos^{2}{(6 x )}&=9 y^{2} \\cos^{2}{(6 x )} - 36 y \\cos{(6 x )} + 36 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}&=- 36 y \\cos{(6 x )} + 36 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\underbrace{(36 \\cos{(6 x )})}_{P(x)}y&=\\underbrace{36}_{Q(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=36 \\cos{(6 x )}$ and $Q(x)=36.$",
